      <p class="byline">Battery Basics · Terminals · Last reviewed 2026-09-23</p>
      <h1>Battery Terminal Types: A Beginner's Guide to the Four Families</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The terminal is where the battery meets the car — two metal posts that carry the entire electrical
        load of the vehicle. Get the family wrong and the battery will not connect; get the fit wrong and the
        connection slowly fails even though the battery is fine.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Four families dominate: <strong>SAE post</strong> (round, tapered,
        most cars), <strong>JIS</strong> (thin tapered posts, Japanese and Asian cars), <strong>stud/bolt</strong>
        (threaded posts, trucks and marine) and <strong>side terminal</strong> (flush bolts, GM-style). The
        vehicle's cables decide which one fits — the terminal, like the
        <a href="/lead-acid/battery-group-sizes/">group size</a>, is a fit requirement, not a preference. A
        deeper dive with identification photos and corrosion mechanics is on
        <a href="/lead-acid/battery-terminal-types/">Battery terminal types: SAE, JIS, stud and side</a>.
      </div>

      <h2>Why terminals exist</h2>
      <p>
        A battery stores chemical energy and releases it as <a href="/basics/voltage-amperage/">electrical
        current</a>. The terminals are the only path for that current to leave the battery — so the
        connection must be tight, clean and large enough to carry the full cranking current without heating.
        A loose or corroded terminal behaves like a narrow pipe: the battery holds the power, but the
        connection will not deliver it.
      </p>

      <h2>The four families, one by one</h2>
      <h3>1. SAE post (the automotive standard)</h3>
      <p>
        The round, tapered posts on top of most car batteries — the shape every beginner pictures. The
        <strong>positive post is thicker</strong> than the negative, a deliberate anti-misconnection feature
        that makes the cable clamps physically unable to fit the wrong way. North American and European cars
        overwhelmingly use SAE posts.
      </p>
      <h3>2. JIS terminal (the thin taper)</h3>
      <p>
        Japanese and Korean vehicles use pencil-thin tapered posts that look like slimmed-down SAE terminals.
        The positive is again the thicker of the pair. A JIS post will not accept an SAE clamp properly — a
        common mistake when an imported battery meets a domestic cable set.
      </p>
      <h3>3. Stud / bolt terminals (threaded)</h3>
      <p>
        A threaded metal post held by a nut, common on trucks, marine and industrial batteries. The eyelet
        lugs on the vehicle's cables slide over the stud and are bolted down — a robust connection that
        tolerates vibration better than a clamp. See
        <a href="/lead-acid/truck-battery/">Truck battery</a> for the systems that standardise on them.
      </p>
      <h3>4. Side terminals (GM-style)</h3>
      <p>
        Instead of posts on top, the connection is a threaded bolt hole in the side wall, into which the
        cable's bolt screws. GM introduced the layout to keep the top of the battery clear. The terminal type
        must match the vehicle — there is no adapter-free interchange between top-post and side-terminal
        designs.
      </p>

      <h2>Polarity and orientation: the other half of the fit</h2>
      <p>
        Even among SAE-post batteries, the <strong>orientation</strong> differs: the positive post can sit on
        the left or the right when the battery faces you. Buying the mirror image of the original battery
        means the existing cables may not reach. The three fit checks — group size, terminal family, terminal
        orientation — are all on the <a href="/lead-acid/battery-size-selection/">What size battery does my
        car need?</a> page, and all three must match before the battery is a fit.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrosion: the terminal's natural enemy</h2>
      <p>
        Terminals corrode because acid vapour and moisture meet metal and air, forming a crust that raises
        <a href="/technology/internal-resistance/">internal resistance</a> at the connection. Symptoms are
        classic: slow cranking on a healthy battery, charging faults, or a car that occasionally refuses to
        start. The fix is cleaning and protection, not a new battery — the full maintenance procedure and the
        chemistry of the deposits are on <a href="/lead-acid/terminal-corrosion/">Terminal corrosion: why it
        happens and how to fix it</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What procurement buyers should check</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Does the quotation state the <strong>terminal family and orientation</strong> — or just the group size? All three must match the end user's vehicle.</li>
        <li>For mixed fleets, is the <strong>terminal spread</strong> (SAE vs JIS vs stud across the fleet) understood, so the right variants are ordered per vehicle class?</li>
        <li>Are the <strong>posts sized for the clamps</strong> — SAE vs JIS taper differences are millimetres that decide whether the clamp seats properly?</li>
        <li>Does the specification include <strong>terminal protection</strong> (grease or coatings) for corrosive environments?</li>
        <li>Is the physical data cross-checked against the group reference — for example the <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/h6/">H6</a> and <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/h7/">H7</a> group pages on the data sub-site list terminal layout per group?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What are the four main battery terminal types?</h3>
      <p>SAE post (the round tapered posts on most cars, positive thicker than negative), JIS (the pencil-thin tapered posts on Japanese and Asian cars), stud/bolt (threaded posts, common on trucks and marine batteries), and side terminal (flush GM-style bolts on the battery side). The vehicle's cable clamps are built for exactly one of them.</p>
      <h3>Does the terminal type matter when buying a replacement battery?</h3>
      <p>Yes — it is a fit requirement, like group size. The existing cable clamps match one terminal family, and a battery with a different terminal type will not connect without adapters or cable work. Match the terminal type and orientation to the old battery and the vehicle.</p>
      <h3>Why do terminals corrode and how do I prevent it?</h3>
      <p>Acid vapour and moisture react with the metal, forming a corrosive deposit that raises connection resistance. Prevention: keep the terminals clean and dry, use protective coating, and make sure the connections are tight. Corroded terminals cause slow cranking and charging faults even when the battery itself is healthy.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Terminal families and fit conventions cross-checked against BCI group specifications and vehicle
        electrical practice; no quoted figures.
      </p>
